#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ee4cfc-dad1-5346-9bd2-a61b7d139e54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/ef9ada6ab016d70b2b14d53cb2f3021793ea78dd69048700c07d5a5161901ecf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362875} {:answer/id #uuid "7c760781-4e93-5dde-8d5e-d42dccf9d706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/c697a6b80923ffb6c557d59aa2e2924561121c373b4221709e280e5d35297e33.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362876} {:answer/id #uuid "9e652e9e-898d-5d56-abe8-96cc47e181cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362877} {:answer/id #uuid "8b94263f-3bb0-5fcb-a57f-181bf6a67121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/c7e7fd7cbf8c3f2f9b68f0af3ecfbdb616730dc7b7cc3b03aa6f35228843080e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362878} {:answer/id #uuid "e8562a1e-14d3-5062-b9b5-794e11b25e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/514e6bec32ef0d172c5d3e9df05da8613806bb60faee267702ffa17238d0f826.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e652e9e-898d-5d56-abe8-96cc47e181cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362877} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19d8b66-dfce-56e2-9a72-0d74061e4f9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df140281-2a89-52e8-9118-441fdf457f4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-340357" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=1$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=1$ (already drawn by the above property) and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points downward, the arrow just below it points downward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/b2cb0682ba91ac23d418d91e7564cbb6245bf498de79b3e615f3ed2b1fcdb997.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/74032d1b543a7446e87b9d3c9b1737f61996cf32817a3bce4db26f229457a348.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=1,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=1$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbcf76d-9e9d-58c5-90a9-c1e96db05314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362882} {:answer/id #uuid "d8778424-d524-588d-9cdf-12d688db404b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362883} {:answer/id #uuid "d7814eb7-87a9-50fb-8f95-12bcdb11eb64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362884} {:answer/id #uuid "d222cc4f-3028-5396-84d4-2afb969d0d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362885} {:answer/id #uuid "abdbb513-2325-5722-a603-35c02cffa98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d222cc4f-3028-5396-84d4-2afb969d0d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362885} :answer-field/id #uuid "51e275b2-51af-57bd-8226-56df154e40cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "652ae185-a5d7-57a7-8788-52d6c37dcf05" :question/math-academy-id "q-98714" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/6b9b15bd3c8cda84156d5461fb9f80f892a1dba26151cc37d381757f9f659fa1.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node.\n- $y=2$ is a sink.\n- $y=2$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/362ffe8871efa8dc597708b039fa766c3603777ba12460e84d9af1fc39800288.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a node.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf1d81fa-d4d5-5629-9d87-ffb57d181d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y =-4$ and $y = 2$ are both sources, and $y =-2$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147068} {:answer/id #uuid "c62c8df7-f14e-533e-87ac-d99e5036fd1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y =-4$ and $y = 2$ are both sinks, and $y =-2$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147069} {:answer/id #uuid "c67a6f20-d005-5e80-92b4-4df20b040168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y =-4$ is a sink, and $y =-2$ and $y = 2$ are both sources" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147070} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb0fa6a-f5b0-5da0-bf65-d9cffa4afb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y =-4$ is a source, and $y =-2$ and $y = 2$ are both sinks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147071} {:answer/id #uuid "126d96e8-5bb7-5a78-9417-2f79813d22c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y =-4$ and $y = 2$ are both sources, and $y =-2$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf1d81fa-d4d5-5629-9d87-ffb57d181d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y =-4$ and $y = 2$ are both sources, and $y =-2$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147068} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd08f49-ca2b-5693-8458-c398156256af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b67f2df0-9c78-5e5b-a84a-57c3b8a1a1ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-98759" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y+4)({y}^{2}-4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y+4)({y}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ (y+4)(y+2)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,-2,2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-4$ and $y=\\pm 2.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,-2)$ | $(-2,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-4,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (-2,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/8756839e7b9c77934acec128d098eab5b7482a3b7422945b3507b6379fbc528b.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-4$ and $y=2$ are both sources, and $y=-2$ is a sink\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c57fca0-ad47-5a39-9685-12b0caf84936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362889} {:answer/id #uuid "602e9dad-fd87-552f-a885-41fd418c4ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a source and $y=-3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362890} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d69e75-153b-5920-a06c-7a97f6f1182c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362891} {:answer/id #uuid "1b93c892-3c7a-57bc-af13-50ffdc5216cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a sink and $y=-3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362892} {:answer/id #uuid "378885b6-f7f8-5300-9346-713e40f1f6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "378885b6-f7f8-5300-9346-713e40f1f6a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362893} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c364d6-5900-5a3f-a3f0-86599d32d657" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2466e8a8-bc2f-5b3d-8b9e-e87c3e64e5e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-98761" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+3y+9$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/244a46b17ad69e8d7cc3ae4f3d9c3c46c1d6e6a7ea154b849946f8ece6030071.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=3$ is a source\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4937e315-9ae8-5299-b59a-1b114d13ce16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/1a9eb4ad4a219ae7b36e418fd51ab15de36940a67b5a8a4aeeef6538e18e551c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362896} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc29a37-6218-5734-b208-7a2e0c43bf44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/b6bbed5a54701e3e467e62fc1acfff78607c63b9d2fad07e092c826a3bca0c0e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362897} {:answer/id #uuid "e878ccef-3a0d-511a-aad6-13c93a91e734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/8fd918e5eba15cec6472f84c64f6181920e8d02122919f844678f79aa23e27a0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362898} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea6c826-1756-5ea7-a61e-a05c68abb455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/fa06ae341ff2c761aa3e60de7270bd0ffad885b7e602c12c7af340acc0781818.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362899} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4b4c90-b402-51bf-8225-fc7e6d89e1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b4b4c90-b402-51bf-8225-fc7e6d89e1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362900} :answer-field/id #uuid "c23821b1-f6e3-5c88-af42-af4beffacd33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55d1795f-d6ac-5ff7-a9de-20bb6292950d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98781" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=0$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=0$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=0$ points downward, the arrow just above it points downward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/db7e9ea076c3a1fe2cf5341b841693e36d5b9a9030e624da88d2cfd3b0d3a1b0.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line."}]}
